      "content": "That's helpful for the students to see how the language worked. One thing they're stuck on is the \"economic\" side of the argument. There were some pretty complex papers at the time about how segregation was supposedly better for the labor market and general societal efficiency, right? \n\nCould you elaborate on those arguments? Specifically, how did they frame the idea that racial separation helped maintain certain economic standards or prevented competition from disrupting the established social order? They really need to understand the \"rational\" economic reasoning used at the time to be effective in the debate.",
