    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proponents of segregation argued that racial separation was necessary to maintain economic efficiency and social order by preventing competition between different racial groups. They claimed that integration would lead to economic disruption and \"racial competition\" for jobs, which would drive down wages and create social unrest.\n\nThey cited studies that supposedly showed that African Americans were less productive and more prone to crime when integrated into white society. For example, they pointed to the \"Chicago School\" of economics, which argued that urbanization and immigration led to increased crime and social disorder. They claimed that segregation was necessary to preserve the economic stability of white communities by preventing the influx of \"undesirable\" racial elements.\n\nThey also invoked the concept of \"natural economic order,\" which posited that different racial groups had different economic needs and abilities. They argued that segregation allowed for the creation of separate labor markets, where each racial group could compete for jobs and resources without disrupting the established social order.\n\nOne specific economic argument made by proponents of segregation was that it helped maintain the \"natural hierarchy\" of the labor market. They claimed that African Americans were best suited to menial and unskilled labor, while whites were better suited to more prestigious and well-paying jobs. By segregating the labor market, they argued,",
